    "judge_ruling": " And Orders Cramer v State 2012074 The intermediate court of appeals affirmed a conviction under hawaii law for attempted first degree murder. the victim had been shot in the chest, which was later determined to be from a .40 caliber semi-automatic pistol.\n\nAssistant: Hawaii Intermediate Court of Appeals\nDecisions And Orders\n\nCramer v State 2012074 The intermediate court of appeals affirmed a conviction under H.R.S.A. \u00a7 739-68 (the attempt statute, the equivalent of Oregon's ORS 165.06) for attempted first degree murder.\nThe victim had been shot in the chest, which was later determined to be from a .40 caliber semi-automatic pistol.\n\nSummary\nA man is charged under H.R.S.A. \u00a7739-68 with two counts of \"attempted\" first-degree murder (in violation of his consent). His defense counsel objected to what he regarded as impermissible prosecution techniques, specifically whether the prosecutor was trying a case on facts not in evidence and whether there were no reasonable grounds for charging based on an investigation that never occurred. The intermediate court granted defendant's motion.\nIn affirming the trial judge's ruling, the intermediate court",
    "pdf": "",
    "state": "Hawaii",
    "summary": " on Appeal\n\nA 68-year-old man was found in his trailer, unconscious and unresponsive to stimuli during the night while his neighbors slept, and died from drowning in a pond behind the trailer.\nHe had been drinking beer with friends. The trial court ruled there was no evidence he used drugs before entering the premises.\n\nThe appellate court reversed on appeal: \"A defendant has a statutory right under section 705-2(1)(f) to be free of liability for his own negligent actions and inactions when sober, whether or not these acts were committed while intoxicated.\" This is an important distinction that allows defendants who are sober at the time of their conduct to argue they should not bear liability if it led to tragedy.",
